'''Opal, S.A.''' is a Balboan {{w|synthetic fiber}} manufacturer and exporter based in [[Sant Bart]]. The company produces {{w|polyester}}, {{w|nylon}}, and {{w|acrylic fiber}} for use in {{w|textile manufacturing}}, supplying both domestic textile producers and export markets across the [[BTPMEU]] region and beyond. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker OPA and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

Opal sources its petrochemical feedstocks — {{w|purified terephthalic acid}}, {{w|ethylene glycol}}, {{w|caprolactam}}, and {{w|acrylonitrile}} — from the [[Badia Curta]] chemical complex, where several of the company's suppliers, including [[CIEA]], [[Grup Rojo]], and [[Companyia Química Occidental]], operate production facilities.

== History ==

Opal was founded in 1986 by Marta Vidal i Soler, a chemical engineer who had previously worked at [[Companyia Química Occidental]]'s [[Badia Curta]] complex. Vidal identified an opportunity to manufacture synthetic fibers domestically using feedstocks available from the Badia Curta petrochemical cluster, reducing Balboa's dependence on imported textile fibers.

The company built its first production facility in [[Sant Bart]], with initial output focused on polyester fiber for the domestic textile industry. The facility's location in the interior provided access to both the Badia Curta feedstock supply chain by rail and the agricultural textile markets of the interior provinces.

In the 1990s, Opal expanded into nylon and acrylic fiber production, broadening its product range to serve a wider segment of the textile manufacturing sector. The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 opened export markets in Tapuya and Potocsí for Balboan manufactured goods, and Opal began exporting significant volumes of synthetic fiber to textile producers across the economic union.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1994. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] acquired a strategic stake in 2003 as part of its broader investment in the domestic processing chain.

== Operations ==

Opal operates a single production complex in [[Sant Bart]], comprising polymerisation, spinning, and texturing facilities for polyester, nylon, and acrylic fiber. The complex receives feedstocks from the [[Badia Curta]] chemical cluster by rail and road.

The company's products are used in a range of textile applications, including apparel, home furnishings, industrial fabrics, and technical textiles. Approximately 55 per cent of output is exported, primarily to [[Tapuya]], [[Potocsí]], and [[Balisca]], with the remainder supplying domestic textile manufacturers.

